<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nyenskans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1611–1703</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Nyenschantz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nyenskans was a Swedish fortress built in 1611 at the confluence of the Okhta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> rivers, on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. It guarded the town of Nyen, a trading post for commerce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and was the centre of Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> captured it in May 1703 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and founded Saint Petersburg nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyenskans.jpg" alt="Nyenskans" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Evgeny Gerashchenko · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyenschantz_model.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
